1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:06,568
Gummy's data table now does what teams reach for AG Grid to do, and all of it is configuration.

2
00:00:07,518 --> 00:00:13,648
Prices, stock and dates arrive as raw numbers. Each column's format shows them in the app's locale.

3
00:00:13,548 --> 00:00:20,181
Style rules tint cells by a condition: out of stock in red, low stock in amber, top ratings in green.

4
00:00:20,631 --> 00:00:25,336
The Columns menu shows or hides a column, and you drag one into a new place.

5
00:00:25,892 --> 00:00:31,247
The layout is remembered, so a reload keeps it, and Reset puts the configured one back.

6
00:00:32,902 --> 00:00:35,690
Tick rows, or every matching row at once.

7
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:42,668
The picks become page data, so any component can read them, like this summary line.

8
00:00:43,382 --> 00:00:49,092
Group three hundred orders by status, then city, with counts, sums and a total row.

9
00:00:48,992 --> 00:00:52,739
And regroup them from the same menu, without touching the config.

10
00:00:54,859 --> 00:00:59,362
Export writes the table as you see it: searched, sorted and formatted.

11
00:00:59,955 --> 00:01:03,158
Into a CSV file that opens straight in Excel.

12
00:01:04,264 --> 00:01:10,085
Scroll mode puts every row in one scroller. Past sixty rows, only the rows in view are drawn.

13
00:01:11,998 --> 00:01:15,021
So three hundred orders scroll as smoothly as ten.

14
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:26,099
Every one of these is a setting on the table, in Gummy Studio.

15
00:01:25,999 --> 00:01:32,317
Columns, selection, style rules, grouping, export and paging, with nothing to code.

16
00:01:34,305 --> 00:01:38,595
Gummy UI. Configure the grid, and keep your code for the business.
